/25","text":"GEOL 3300 or instructor's consent"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"GEOL 7318: Environmental Soil Chemistry","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":218.79999365000003,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":36.0,"r":197.38400000000004,"t":226.19999365},"charspan":[0,39],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/26","text":"GEOL 7318: Environmental Soil Chemistry"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(same as SOIL 7318 and ENV_SC 7318; cross-leveled with GEOL 4318, SOIL 4318, ENV_SC 4318). Study of chemical constituents and processes occurring in soils. Topics include soil minerals, and weathering processes organic matter, solution chemistry, oxidation-reduction reactions and adsorption processes.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":163.79999365000003,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":36.0,"r":295.64000000000016,"t":215.19999365},"charspan":[0,302],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/27","text":"(same as SOIL 7318 and ENV_SC 7318; cross-leveled with GEOL 4318, SOIL 4318, ENV_SC 4318). Study of chemical constituents and processes occurring in soils. Topics include soil minerals, and weathering processes organic matter, solution chemistry, oxidation-reduction reactions and adsorption processes."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Credit Hours : 3","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":147.79999365000003,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":36.0,"r":93.34400185999999,"t":155.19999365},"charspan":[0,16],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/28","text":"Credit Hours : 3"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Prerequisites: SOIL 2100 or GEOL 2400, CHEM 1320 and CHEM 1330 or instructor's consent","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":125.79999365000003,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":36.0,"r":293.0080011900001,"t":144.19999365},"charspan":[0,86],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/29","text":"Prerequisites: SOIL 2100 or GEOL 2400, CHEM 1320 and CHEM 1330 or instructor's consent"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"GEOL 7350: Taphonomy","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":99.79999365000003,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":36.0,"r":128.912,"t":107.19999365000001},"charspan":[0,20],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/30","text":"GEOL 7350: Taphonomy"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(cross-leveled with GEOL 4350). The purposes of this course are to 1) analyze patterns in the history of life and 2) to recognize the biased processes that led to its preservation. We will accomplish these goals by examining two disparate fossil preservation pathways: 1) Konservat lagerstätten: fossil deposits that are notorious for the exceptional and rare preservation of soft tissues and 2) Konzentrat lagerstätten: fossil deposits that are exceptional for the enormous amount of fossilized skeletal material they contain. Through careful examination of both the rare and hyper-abundant we can address the fundamental question of paleobiology: how literally can the fossil record be read as the history of life? Graded on A-F basis only.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":44.79999365000003,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":36.0,"r":285.432,"t":96.19999365000001},"charspan":[0,354],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":637.8000012800001,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":315.0,"r":568.0,"t":700.19999985},"charspan":[355,742],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/31","text":"(cross-leveled with GEOL 4350). The purposes of this course are to 1) analyze patterns in the history of life and 2) to recognize the biased processes that led to its preservation. We will accomplish these goals by examining two disparate fossil preservation pathways: 1) Konservat lagerstätten: fossil deposits that are notorious for the exceptional and rare preservation of soft tissues and 2) Konzentrat lagerstätten: fossil deposits that are exceptional for the enormous amount of fossilized skeletal material they contain. Through careful examination of both the rare and hyper-abundant we can address the fundamental question of paleobiology: how literally can the fossil record be read as the history of life? Graded on A-F basis only."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Credit Hours : 3","parent":{"$ref":"#/groups/3"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":621.80000128,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":315.0,"r":372.34400186,"t":629.20000128},"charspan":[0,16],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/32","text":"Credit Hours : 3"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"GEOL 7370: Conservation Paleobiology","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":595.80000128,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":315.0,"r":465.71200000000005,"t":603.20000128},"charspan":[0,36],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/33","text":"GEOL 7370: Conservation Paleobiology"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(cross-leveled with GEOL 4370). Humans are having an undeniable influence on Earth systems, including the biosphere. Climate change, habitat destruction, over-hunting/harvesting, pollution, and invasive species are among the primary stressors of modern biodiversity. How have ecosystems responded to such stressors in the past? How do we determine what a \"pristine\" ecosystem looks like when planning ecosystem restoration? Paleontologists are uniquely equipped to address these questions using the fossil record. In this course we will review the important questions in this emerging discipline, learn about the tools used to address these questions, and learn from case studies involving invertebrate, vertebrate, and plant fossils from a variety of environments.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":474.79999365000003,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":315.0,"r":574.6800000000001,"t":592.20000128},"charspan":[0,765],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/34","text":"(cross-leveled with GEOL 4370). Humans are having an undeniable influence on Earth systems, including the biosphere. Climate change, habitat destruction, over-hunting/harvesting, pollution, and invasive species are among the primary stressors of modern biodiversity. How have ecosystems responded to such stressors in the past? How do we determine what a \"pristine\" ecosystem looks like when planning ecosystem restoration? Paleontologists are uniquely equipped to address these questions using the fossil record. In this course we will review the important questions in this emerging discipline, learn about the tools used to address these questions, and learn from case studies involving invertebrate, vertebrate, and plant fossils from a variety of environments."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Credit Hours : 3","parent":{"$ref":"#/groups/4"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":458.79999365000003,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":315.0,"r":372.34400186,"t":466.19999365},"charspan":[0,16],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/35","text":"Credit Hours : 3"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"GEOL 7500: Organic Geochemistry","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":432.79999365,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":315.0,"r":448.38400000000007,"t":440.19999365},"charspan":[0,31],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/36","text":"GEOL 7500: Organic Geochemistry"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(cross-leveled with GEOL 4500). Topics include chemistry of petroleumforming reactions and their kinetic parameters; use of organic-chemical criteria in source-rock evaluation; carbon isotope fractionation in organic precursors of biological molecules; early history of earth's atmosphere.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":388.79999365,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":315.0,"r":569.768,"t":429.19999365},"charspan":[0,289],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/37","text